{"/api/discogs/artist/11538098/profile":{"cached":true,"profile":{"id":"11538098","image":"","imageThumb":"","profile":"Blaqbonez is a Nigerian rapper, singer, and songwriter.","provider":"discogs","ref":"discogs:artist:11538098","relations":{"aliases":null,"groups":null,"members":null,"namevariations":null},"schemaVersion":2,"sourceUrl":"https://www.discogs.com/artist/11538098-Blaqbonez","type":"artist","urls":["https://trackloaded.com/tag/blaqbonez/"]}},"/api/discogs/artist/12596/profile":{"cached":true,"profile":{"id":"12596","image":"https://i.discogs.com/iTYFVrfFoWenGCrdWDkPDt16OPico2pXXWXYgPRErrk/rs:fit/g:sm/q:90/h:479/w:600/czM6Ly9kaXNjb2dz/LWRhdGFiYXNlLWlt/YWdlcy9BLTEyNTk2/LTE1NjU5OTY3MzEt/NTQzMi5qcGVn.jpeg","imageThumb":"https://i.discogs.com/mqxqfWfllCYZJzNUTVhGvN0mqZhcpA8vFVREuO74F60/rs:fit/g:sm/q:40/h:150/w:150/czM6Ly9kaXNjb2dz/LWRhdGFiYXNlLWlt/YWdlcy9BLTEyNTk2/LTE1NjU5OTY3MzEt/NTQzMi5qcGVn.jpeg","profile":"American singer, songwriter, and musician (born 3 May 1933 near Barnwell, South Carolina; died 25 December 2006 in Atlanta, Georgia (aged 73).\n\nPlease do no confuse with [a4026794]\n\nRaised in poverty in Augusta, Georgia. Prior to his career in music, he worked as a shoe-shiner, car washer, and cotton-picker.\n\n1950s: Joined the Gospel Starlighters in 1953, a vocal quartet led by [a=Bobby Byrd], after completing a four-year stint in prison for robbery. As Brown became the focal point of the act, the group changed its name to 'The Famous Flames' and its focus from gospel to R&B. In 1955 The Famous Flames record \"Please Please Please\" at the studio of WIBB in Macon, Georgia. In 1958 James Brown's first #1 hit, \"Try Me\", is released. It was the best-selling R&B single of 1958, and the first of 17 chart-topping R&B singles by Brown over the next two decades.\n\n1965: Records \"Papa's Got a Brand New Bag\", a revolutionary single that ushers in a whole new era of soul music. Released that summer, it tops the R&B chart for eight weeks and even cracks the pop Top Ten.\n\n1971: Signs with Polydor Records, for which he recorded extensively throughout the decade. \n1974: The Payback, the most successful of James Brown's seventies albums—many of which were double-LPs with lengthy, extended tracks—makes its debut on Billboard's album chart. It is the only gold-certified (500,000 copies sold) album of his career.\n\n1984: Bronx DJ [a=Afrika Bambaataa] teams up with Brown to record the single \"Unity\".\n1986: \"Living In America\", the theme song from Rocky IV, reaches #4 on Billboard's Hot 100 chart, becoming Brown's biggest pop hit since \"I Got You (I Feel Good)\" reached #3 in 1965.\n\n1992: Receives a Lifetime Achievement Award at the 34th annual Grammy Awards.\n\n2006: Hospitalized with pneumonia, died on Christmas Day.\n\nInducted into Rock And Roll Hall of Fame in 1986 (Performer).\nInducted into Songwriters Hall of Fame in 2000.","provider":"discogs","ref":"discogs:artist:12596","relations":{"aliases":[{"id":642393,"name":"Ted Wright"},{"id":882323,"name":"Dessie Rozier"},{"id":3239210,"name":"Jim Jam (3)"},{"id":4195384,"name":"The Minister Of New New Super Heavy Funk"},{"id":4842780,"name":"The Godfather Of Soul"},{"id":5221125,"name":"B. Newton"},{"id":5221134,"name":"James Who ?"},{"id":8234302,"name":"B. Jones (8)"}],"groups":[{"active":true,"id":30182,"name":"Fred Wesley & The JB's"},{"active":true,"id":61402,"name":"The J.B.'s"},{"active":true,"id":227924,"name":"The Last Word"},{"active":true,"id":227925,"name":"The First Family"},{"active":true,"id":227926,"name":"The James Brown Soul Train"},{"active":true,"id":258004,"name":"Nat Kendrick & The Swans"},{"active":true,"id":287209,"name":"Friends Of Earth"},{"active":false,"id":332598,"name":"The Famous Flames"},{"active":true,"id":332609,"name":"The James Brown Band"},{"active":true,"id":386724,"name":"James Brown & The Famous Flames"},{"active":true,"id":398279,"name":"The Poets (5)"},{"active":true,"id":418195,"name":"J.B.'s Internationals"},{"act